

Short Explanation:
the model is a small ResNet-style image classifier trained from scratch.
It receives a 224x224 RGB image and predicts one of the 20 target classes.

We chose a small ResNet because it gives a good balance between accuracy,
training stability, and model size. A simple CNN can learn basic image features,
but when the network becomes deeper it can become harder to train. ResNet-style
skip connections help with this problem: each block can keep useful information
from earlier layers and only learn the needed correction. This makes the
training more stable and helps the model learn both simple patterns, such as
edges and colors, and more meaningful object features, such as shape and object
parts.

We preferred a smaller ResNet over a very large model because the dataset is
limited to 20 classes. A very large model could overfit the training images,
especially because we train from random initialization and cannot use pretrained
weights. The small ResNet is still strong enough to learn useful visual
features, but it is easier to train and less likely to overfit compared to a
much larger architecture.

Hyperparameters:
We tuned the main hyperparameters by trial and error and selected the model
according to clean validation performance.
The learning rate controls how large each update step is during training. We
used Adam with a learning rate of 0.001, because it gave stable improvement
during training and did not require very delicate manual tuning.

The batch size controls how many images are used before each optimizer update.
We used a batch size of 128 for all our training runs, as it optimized GPU
utilization and made the gradient estimate smoother while generalizing well.
The number of epochs controls how many times the model sees the training set.

We split our training into two distinct phases (30 epochs for the baseline,
followed by 15 epochs for augmented training). Instead of simply using the
weights from the last epoch of each run, we saved the model whenever the
validation accuracy improved. This way, the final weights.joblib file
contains the strictly best model found during training.

We used CrossEntropyLoss because this is a multi-class classification problem:
each image belongs to exactly one of the 20 possible classes.

Baseline experiment:
Before adding augmentations, we first trained a baseline model without extra
data augmentations. The goal was to verify that the architecture can learn the
20 classes from the clean training images. We trained this baseline model for
30 epochs with a batch size of 128.

This baseline stage was important because it gave us a stable reference point.
It helped us understand whether performance problems came from the model itself
or from the robustness strategy. After this initial run, the baseline model
reached an impressive 81.17% accuracy on our clean local evaluation set.

Data manipulations and augmentation strategy:
After establishing the baseline, we continued with augmentation experiments.
Instead of starting from scratch, we loaded the best weights achieved during
the baseline run and resumed training on the augmented dataset for an
additional 15 epochs (using the same batch size of 128).

The idea was to build upon the basic visual structure the model had already
learned from the clean samples, and make it more robust to visual changes
that should not change the class label.

The main manipulations we used were image transformations such as rotations,
horizontal flips, changes in scale or crop, shifts, and color or brightness
changes. These augmentations simulate natural changes in images, such as a
slightly different object position, lighting condition, or camera crop.

We avoided applying too many extreme augmentations together to prevent the
training images from becoming too distorted. The goal was to expose the model
to controlled visual variation so that it learns to focus more on the object
itself and less on shortcuts such as background, lighting, or color.

Results and observations:
Baseline clean local evaluation accuracy: 81.17%
The baseline model already achieved strong clean evaluation performance. This
means the chosen architecture was able to learn the 20 target classes well
without relying on external data or pretrained weights.

Augmentations were used as a robustness strategy. By fine-tuning our strong
baseline model with augmented data, we aimed to improve stability on images
with changes in color, lighting, crop, or geometry.

Final selected model: Augmented model (fine-tuned from baseline)
Augmented accuracy: 85.3%


